# Keyed one-shot timers: a transaction registers an instant for a key, and the feed `fired` emits
# the key once the clock passes that instant.
#
# A run of this module holds a set of timers, at most one pending per key. A timer is pending from
# the `register` that sets it until it fires or a `cancel` ends it. `register`, `cancel` and
# `pending` act in the caller's transaction, and `fired` is a feed the run's own loop feeds. A loop
# over `fired` handles each firing in a transaction of its own.
#
# The module declares state, so a module runs it and never imports it (docs/chl-spec.md, "9.7
# Importing asserts no IO and no state"). Each run's timers are its own (docs/chl-spec.md, "9.9
# Running"), so two runs never see each other's keys, whatever their key types.
#
# Example, sessions that expire 30 minutes after their last request, and end early on logout:
#
#     import std::time
#
#     run std::time::timer(Key=String) as expiries
#
#     sessions: Mut(Map(String, time::Instant), Txn) := empty_map()   # session id -> expiry
#     expired = defer()                                                # Feed({session: String})
#
#     for r in requests:
#         with begin():
#             expiry = time::now() + time::minutes(30)
#             sessions[r.session] := expiry
#             expiries::register(r.session, expiry)
#
#     for r in logouts:
#         with begin():
#             expiries::cancel(r.session)
#             sessions[r.session] := time::now()
#
#     for f in expiries::fired:
#         with begin():
#             if sessions[f.key]? == Option::some(f.at):
#                 expired << (session=f.key)
#
# A session is open while its expiry is in the future, so a logout ends it by moving its expiry to
# the present. Each request moves its session's timer by registering a new instant, which replaces
# the pending one, so a session has one pending timer however many requests it sees. A firing
# reaches its loop after the timer fired, and a request can register a new expiry in between, so
# the loop compares the firing's instant with the session's current expiry before it reports the
# session expired.
#
# Status: proposed, not in the spec. The std root it belongs to is [Planned] (docs/chl-spec.md,
# "9.16 The std root"). `[proposed]` marks each construct the compiler lacks.

import std::time

# The key type of this run's timers.
#
# [proposed] the requirement `Equatable(Key)`, which a `Map` key needs, on a module's type parameter
# (docs/chl-spec.md, "9.4 Parameters", Open).
param Key

# One firing: the key, and the instant its timer was set to.
pub Firing = {key: Key, at: time::Instant}

# Each key's latest timer: the instant it is set to, in `instants`, and whether it is still pending,
# in `live`. A timer that fires or is cancelled stays with `live` false, since a map entry cannot be
# removed; `pending`, `cancel` and the loop over `scheduled` read such a key as having no timer.
# Once the loop over `scheduled` has passed a key's instant and the key is not live, nothing
# distinguishes it from an absent key, so the runtime may release both entries.
instants: Mut(Map(Key, time::Instant), Txn) := empty_map()
live: Mut(Map(Key, Bool), Txn) := empty_map()

# Every timer `register` sets, including ones a later `register` or `cancel` ends before they fire.
#
# [proposed] a feed private to a module (docs/chl-spec.md, "9.8 Module types", lists public feeds).
scheduled = defer()   # Feed(Firing)

# Sets the timer for `key` to `at`, replacing the key's pending timer if it has one.
#
# A replaced timer never fires. An instant already passed fires as soon as the transaction commits.
# A key whose timer has fired takes a new timer like any other key, including one set to the
# instant it fired at.
#
# [proposed] `requires Transaction` (docs/chl-spec.md, "8.7 Direction [Decided]: transactions as
# contextual parameters").
pub def register(key: Key, at: time::Instant) requires Transaction:
    instants[key] := at
    live[key] := True
    scheduled << (key=key, at=at)

# Cancels the pending timer for `key`, and returns whether there was one.
#
# A cancelled timer never fires. `cancel` returns `False` when `key` has no pending timer: none was
# registered, it was cancelled already, or it has fired. A timer that has fired cannot be recalled;
# its firing reaches the loops over `fired` whatever the caller does.
#
# [proposed] `requires Transaction`, as `register`.
pub def cancel(key: Key) => Bool requires Transaction:
    was_live = live[key]? == Option::some(True)
    live[key] := False
    was_live

# Returns the instant the pending timer for `key` is set to, or `none` when `key` has no pending
# timer.
#
# [proposed] `requires Transaction`, as `register`.
pub def pending(key: Key) => Option(time::Instant) requires Transaction:
    if live[key]? == Option::some(True):
        instants[key]?
    else:
        Option::none

# Emits `(key=k, at=t)` once the clock passes `t`, for each pending timer.
#
# - **A timer fires once, atomically.** The emission and the end of `k`'s pending timer commit
#   together, so after a firing commits, `pending(k)` is `none` until a new `register`, and
#   `cancel(k)` returns `False`.
# - **Fired, then delivered.** A firing reaches each loop over `fired` after it commits, in that
#   loop's own transaction. A `register` or a `cancel` that commits in between does not recall it.
# - A firing comes at or after its instant, with no upper bound: after downtime, every timer whose
#   instant passed fires at once.
# - Firings of different keys come in no defined order.
# - Pending timers and fired, undelivered firings are durable: a timer pending at a crash or a
#   reload fires after it, and a firing its loops had not released is delivered again. The feed
#   emits at most one element per `(key, at)` pair of a firing.
#
# [proposed] a public feed as a member of a Module type (docs/chl-spec.md, "9.8 Module types").
pub fired = defer()   # Feed(Firing)

# Waits out each scheduled timer, then fires it if it is still the key's pending timer at that
# instant. A timer that a `register` replaced or a `cancel` ended no longer matches, and the loop
# drops it; so does the second of two registrations of one `(key, at)`, which find the first fired.
#
# A loop over `fired` that calls `register` closes a cycle of feeds: that loop feeds `scheduled`,
# this loop reads `scheduled` and feeds `fired`, and that loop reads `fired`. The cycle is causal
# only through `time::wait_until`, which releases an element at a later instant than it arrived.
#
# [proposed] a cycle of feeds whose causal edge is `time::wait_until` (src/ccl/design/mutability.md,
# "`LetRec`": its well-formedness rule admits only a `get_prev_*` call as a causal edge).
for s in scheduled:
    time::wait_until(s.at)
    with begin():
        if instants[s.key]? == Option::some(s.at) and live[s.key]? == Option::some(True):
            live[s.key] := False
            fired << s
